# Environment variables every NSH application can read from
# `process.env` without listing them in `capabilities.process.env`.
#
# One name per line, in upper case; `#` starts a comment. Compiled into the
# runtime by `access.rs`, whose tests check every entry. A name matches
# regardless of case, under the spelling the environment uses.
#
# A variable belongs here only when it is presentation metadata: it tells an
# app how to format text and time, never who the user is, where their files
# live or how to reach anything. So the user and machine names (`USER`,
# `HOME`, `HOSTNAME`, ...), paths (`PATH`, `TMPDIR`, `XDG_*_HOME`,
# `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, `APPDATA`, ...: most embed the user name), proxies
# (`HTTP_PROXY` and friends, whose URLs can carry a user name and password)
# and session addresses (`DISPLAY`, `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`,
# `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`) are left out. An application that needs one asks for it by
# name in its manifest, where the user and the embedding app can see it.

# Language and time.
LANG
LANGUAGE
LC_ALL
LC_ADDRESS
LC_COLLATE
LC_CTYPE
LC_IDENTIFICATION
LC_MEASUREMENT
LC_MESSAGES
LC_MONETARY
LC_NAME
LC_NUMERIC
LC_PAPER
LC_TELEPHONE
LC_TIME
TZ

# The terminal's capabilities (colour output of command-line tools).
TERM
COLORTERM

# The kind of desktop and machine, for following its conventions.
XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
XDG_SESSION_TYPE
DESKTOP_SESSION
PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE
